{
  "abstract": "A machine-checked proof of Gross's discrete Hudson theorem: for odd d, a pure state of n qudits has a non-negative Wigner function if and only if it is a stabilizer state. Every odd d is covered, including composite d, and every n. The definitions follow Gross's Section II: the Weyl operators w(p, q) with the symmetric phase convention, the phase point operators A(a) = d^(-n) sum_b omega([a, b]) w(b), the Wigner function W(a) = d^(-n) <psi|A(a)|psi>, and a stabilizer state as a common eigenvector of the Weyl operators of an isotropic subgroup of phase space with d^n elements. Also proved: the Wigner function is real, and equals the closed form d^(-n) sum_xi omega(p . xi) conj(psi(q + xi/2)) psi(q - xi/2) (Gross, Theorem 6.2, after xi -> -xi), and the equivalence of the definition with Gross's Lemma 8 form, where the eigenvalues are omega([v, m])^(-1). The proof does not follow Gross's Section III; it follows the route of Gross, Nezami and Walter (2021), Section 8.1. For a pure state the Wigner values sum to 1, their squares sum to d^(-n), and none exceeds d^(-n), so non-negative values are 0 or d^(-n); at the d^n points where W = d^(-n) the phase point operators fix the state, and products of two of them give a maximal isotropic group of Weyl operators of which the state is a common eigenvector. The converse is a character sum. Controls show both sides of the equivalence are attained, including at the composite dimension d = 9.",
